(1) When a company is wound up, every present and past member is contribute its assets to any amount sufficient for -

(a) the payment of its debts and liabilities;

(b) the expenses of the winding up; and

(c) the adjustment of the rights of the contributories among themselves.

(2) This is subject as follows(a) a past member is not liable to contribute if he has ceased to be a for one year or more before the commencement of the winding up;

(b) a past member is not liable to contribute 'in respect of any debt or the company contracted after he ceased to be a member;

(c) a past member is not liable to contribute, unless it appears to the that the existing members are unable to satisfy the contributions to be made by them;

(d) in the case of a company limited by shares, no contribution is from any member exceeding the amount (if apy) unpaid on the in respect of which he is liable as a present or past member;

(e) nothing in the Companies Act (Chapter 39) or this Order invalidates any provision contained in a policy of insurance or other whereby the liability of individual members on the policy or contract is restricted, or whereby the funds of the company are alone made liable in respect of the policy or contract;

(f) a sum due to any member of the company (in his character of a by way of dividends, profits or otherwise is not deemed to be a debt of the company, payable to that member in a case of competition himself and any other creditor not a member of the company, but any such sum may be taken into account for the purpose of the final of the rights of the contributories among themselves.

(3) In the case of a company limited by guarantee, no contribution is from any member exceeding the amount undertaken to be contributed by him to the company's assets in the event of its being wound up; but if it is a with a share capital, every member of it is liable (in addition to the so undertaken to be contributed to the assets), to contribute to the extent of any sums unpaid on shares held by him. of past directors and shareholders

